    733:| arXiv:2506.22634 TG-kernel "rigorous bound" for π(x) (Kılıçtaş-Alpay 2025) | FAIL | C+I | Paper claims ~1200 zeros suffice for π(x) at x with 10^8 digits via truncated-Gaussian Φ_TG(t)=e^{-t²} on [0,α] with cubic taper, plugged into Riesz-Weil explicit formula. Empirical falsification (S65, full PDF audited): (a) ∫Φ_TG(t)dt = 0.886 ≠ 0, so paper's premise F_TG(1)=0 (main term cancels) is self-contradictory — Φ_TG ≥ 0 on its support cannot integrate to 0; (b) S(x)=Σ Λ(n)Φ_TG(n/x) measured at x∈{100..30000} grows linearly as ≈0.886·x, NOT αe^{-α²}≈3.7×10⁻⁴ as paper derives — IBP on p.7 substitutes Ψ(t)≈t (PNT) and drops the (Ψ-t) deviation that encodes prime fluctuations, leaving an x-free identity; (c) Σ F_TG(ρ) is x-independent → cannot encode π(x) when "rounded"; (d) Lemma 2 zero-density bound N(σ,T)≤AT^{1-1/σ}(log T)^B at σ=1/2 gives AT^{-1}(log T)^B, decreasing in T, blatantly false (true (T/2π)log T grows); (e) Appendix B is symbolic-mysticism crank content ("EmbedS(Faruk Alpay):=Φ_∞... canonical identity fold"). The "smoothed-kernel beats explicit formula" idea is folklore: smoothing in log-scale with width h gives T·h ~ const, and integer-precision π(x) needs h ≲ log(x)/x ⇒ T ~ x. No polylog escape via this route. See experiments/wildcard/tg_kernel_audit.py | 65 |
    769:| §D6 ATTACK_VECTORS — Gowers U^k uniformity norms of the bare prime indicator chi_P (cross-domain: additive combinatorics, Green-Tao-Ziegler U^{s+1} inverse theorem) | FAIL | E | Frontier attack on whether chi_P deviates from random pseudorandomly under any Gowers norm at k <= 3 — the only family of "k-degree polynomial structure" tests untouched by the project's 35+ pseudorandomness measures. Computed normalized Gowers norms `Q^k(f) := ‖f‖_{U^k}^{2^k} / E[f]^{2^k}` on Z/NZ for chi_P, matched-density Bernoulli, Liouville indicator, and W-tricked chi_{W,1} (W ∈ {6, 30, 210}). U^2 via FFT identity `‖f‖_{U^2}^4 = (1/N^4) Σ_k |fhat(k)|^4` (O(N log N)) at N ∈ {2^10, 2^12, 2^14, 2^16, 2^18}; U^3 via recursion `‖f‖_{U^3}^8 = (1/N) Σ_h ‖Δ_h f‖_{U^2}^4` with `Δ_h f(x) = f(x)f(x+h)` (O(N^2 log N)) at N ∈ {2^10, 2^11, 2^12, 2^13}. **Hardy-Littlewood prediction**: `Q^k(chi_P) → S_k = Π_p α_p(k) / (1 - 1/p)^{2^k}` (singular series for {0,1}^k-cube prime k-tuple), where `α_p(k) = #{(x, h) ∈ (Z/p)^{k+1} : x + ε·h ≢ 0 mod p ∀ε ∈ {0,1}^k} / p^{k+1}`. Numerical truncated values: **S_2 = 2.300938** (P_max=100, 1e-5 truncation error), **S_3 = 54.116088** (P_max=47, 0.2% error). **Empirical Q^2(chi_P) trend**: 2.103 (N=2^10) → 2.132 (N=2^12) → 2.146 (N=2^14) → 2.149 (N=2^16) → 2.153 (N=2^18); monotonic increase toward S_2; gap S_2 - Q^2(chi_P) decays as O(1/log N) (from 0.198 at N=2^10 to 0.148 at N=2^18). **Q^2(Bernoulli) ~ 1 + O(1/N)** (1.049 → 1.008 across same range). **Q^3(chi_P) ≈ 35.5** stable across N ∈ [2^10, 2^13], well above Bernoulli's Q^3 → 1 but below S_3 = 54.12 due to slow finite-N convergence at U^3 (compounded log-factors). **Liouville**: Q^2(L) = 1.000 to 4 digits at N=2^10..2^12 — Gowers-uniform at U^2, consistent with Green-Tao Mobius/nilsequence orthogonality (the prime-parity indicator is structurally OPPOSITE to chi_P at U^k). **W-trick**: predicted `S^(W)_k = Π_{p∤W} α_p(k)/(1-1/p)^{2^k}`. At N=2^12, **W=210**: empirical Q^2(chi_{W,1}) = 1.0029 vs HL prediction 1.0023 (match within 0.1%); empirical Q^3 = 1.066 vs HL 1.029 (match within 4%, dominated by finite-N noise on Q^3). **W=30**: Q^2 = 1.005 vs 1.007, exact match. The W-trick empirically restores Gowers-uniformity to chi_P, exactly as the Green-Tao W-trick predicts for Lambda. **First instance in the project's pseudorandomness battery (after S84 circuit-depth gap at N=6) where chi_P deviates from random by a CLOSED-FORM, predicted, constant multiplicative factor**, with the deviation EXACTLY matching the Hardy-Littlewood box singular series. **Closure mode E**: U^k structure of chi_P provides only HL-level information, already conjecturally known; no additional bit of structure detected; algorithmic extraction of Q^k itself costs Θ(N^2 log N), not polylog. **Cross-domain ingredient performed real work**: Gowers norms (Gowers 2001) detect higher-order correlation structure invisible to the project's local statistics; the FFT identity for U^2 and recursion for U^3 are standard machinery from additive combinatorics. **Cited new EDGE: E2.13** (Q^k(chi_P) = S_k {0,1}^k-cube). Cites E1.10, E3.13, E7.1, E2.9, S84. Cross-domain refs: Gowers (2001); Green-Tao (arXiv:math/0606088); Green-Tao (arXiv:0807.1736); Green-Tao-Ziegler (arXiv:1009.3998); Hardy-Littlewood (1923). See experiments/information_theory/gowers_uk_chi_p_results.md. **S237 re-verify-closure annotation**: closure stands, sharpened. Pointwise spike approximator T_Q (S205/S191) shown rank-AUC equivalent to small-prime wheel sieve W_Q in the asymptotic n > Q window, max |AUC(T_Q) - AUC(W_Q)| ≤ 4×10⁻⁵ across Q ∈ {6, 30, 210, 2310} at N = 30000. The 0.053 AUC gap that appears in the full range n ≥ 2 at Q = 2310 is an edge artefact (T_Q correctly scores small primes p ≤ Q while W_Q sieves them out as multiples of themselves; small-prime band O(Q/log Q) is polylog and trivially enumerable). Rules out a class of "smoothed-wheel + extra structure" exploitations of the Gowers/HL identity. See experiments/constructions/spike_primality_discriminator/. | 85 |
    816:| S202 commit-thread step 5/5 WRAP (Connes amortisation) — synthesise S193–S196 into one statement: do any of {per-query unsmoothed, amortised, CCM-spectral, in-distribution, log-Gaussian-smoothed} regimes break the K = Θ̃(x) per-query floor for π(x) ± 1? | SYNTHESIS+CLOSURE | E | Thread 2 commit step 5/5 WRAP (final synthesis; no new code/experiment). **Unified conditional theorem (Connes-Galway equivalence)**: under the Montgomery pair-correlation random-phase heuristic, for π_{K,h}(x) := R(x) − 2 Σ_{j≤K} Re R(x^{ρ_j}) · exp(−h²γ_j²/2) and any p ∈ (0, 1), the smallest K with Pr(|π(x) − π_{K,h}(x)| ≤ 1/2) ≥ p satisfies K*(x, p, h) = Θ̃(x). Specifically K*(x, 0.5, 0) ≈ 0.0903·x and K*(x, 0.99, 0) ≈ 1.35·x; increasing h ≥ 0 cannot reduce K* (Var_TAIL is h-independent by S196 disjoint-j variance-additivity). Setup-cost asymmetry: CCM dense eigensolver O(K³) vs Hiary 2011 O(K^{17/13}) per-zero locator; ratio K^{22/13} for any Q queries; per-query identical at O(K). **Five regimes simultaneously closed**: (1) per-query unsmoothed (S193 + S195); (2) amortised setup over Q queries (S193); (3) CCM spectral-triple eigenproblem amortised (S193); (4) in-distribution rather than worst-case rounding (S195 with constants 0.0903, 1.35); (5) log-Gaussian-smoothed explicit formula at any bandwidth h ≥ 0 (S196). No regime breaks the floor. **Empirical scope**: 600 unsmoothed (x, K, |err|) triples from S194 + S195 over x ∈ [10^5, 10^7] plus 440 smoothed (x, K, h, |err|) triples from S196 at x ~ 1.78·10^5 across 11 bandwidths. **Closure conditional on the iid-uniform-phase model**; the 26% slope-ratio gap from S195 IS the GUE-vs-Poisson reduction. **Open falsifiers (acknowledged but not needed for Thread 2 closure)**: (a) non-Gaussian smoothing kernels (sinc, sech², Bartlett); (b) empirical extension to x ≥ 10^9; (c) rigorous GUE pair-correlation reduction of K* below x · log^{-c}(x); (d) cross-x amortisation as a different computational model. None known to fail. **Recommended next thread**: all three threads (S82, Connes, Galway) now CLOSED → frontier_gen mode (auto-fire conditions met) OR direct A-grade attempt on A7 plethysm / D44 BC endomotive (S192/S163 flagged). Cites E3.1, E1.5, E2.1, S53 row 706, S193 row 810, S194 row 818, S195 row 816, S196 row 814, Galway 2004, Hiary 2011, Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989. Cross-domain composition: asymmetric setup-cost analysis (S193) + hit-rate-at-fixed-polylog measurement (S194) + GUE random-phase variance estimator with li-asymptotic + Gaussian CLT (S195) + Mellin-domain log-Gaussian kernel (S196), all unified under one conditional theorem. See `archive/sessions/session202_commit_connes_amortisation.md`. | 202 |
    820:| S196 commit-thread step 4 (Connes amortisation, Galway smoothed frontier in distribution) — does Gaussian-window smoothing of the explicit formula drop K* into the polylog regime? | THEORY+EXPERIMENT | E+I | Thread 2 commit step 4/5. **Slot 4 closes Thread 3 under log-Gaussian smoothing**: defining π_{K,h}(x) := R(x) − 2 Σ_{j≤K} Re R(x^{ρ_j}) · exp(−h² γ_j² / 2), the error decomposes into TAIL (j > K, unsmoothed) + BIAS (j ≤ K, weighting bias). Under iid uniform-phase model the two sums are over disjoint j ranges hence variance-additive: Var(error) = Var(TAIL)(K) + Var(BIAS)(K, h) where Var(TAIL)(K) ≈ x · log²(K)/(2π² K log²x) is **independent of h**, and Var(BIAS)(K, h) ≈ x · h · log(1/h)/log²(x) (asymptotic, h γ_K saturated). Var(TAIL)(K) ≤ 1/4 forces K ≥ Θ(x · poly⁻¹(log x)) regardless of h — **smoothing cannot lower K* below Θ̃(x)**. **Empirical validation** (40 samples × 11 bandwidths × K_max=2000 at x ~ 1.78e5): K*_50 = 1783 unsmoothed, identical (1782) for h ∈ {1e-6, 1e-4, 3e-4} (h γ_K_max ≤ 1, smoothing inactive); K*_50 unreachable within K_max for h ≥ 5e-4 (smoothing introduces bias dominating tail savings). Bias variance prediction matches empirical to within 5–15% across active regime: e.g. h=10⁻³ pred σ_BIAS=0.85 vs emp=0.72 (15% off); h=10⁻² pred=3.20 vs emp=3.05 (5% off); h=10⁻¹ pred=6.80 vs emp=6.01 (12% off). **The optimal bandwidth is h = 0**: smoothing strictly does NOT help. Eliminates S195's recommended slot-(b) follow-up. Strengthens S195's closure of Thread 3 from "in-distribution unsmoothed" to "in-distribution for any log-Gaussian bandwidth". Galway 2004 reconciliation: his K = O(x^{1/2+ε}) is for a smoothed *sum output*, not in-distribution recovery of integer π(x); regimes are not in conflict. Cites E3.1, E1.5, S193 row 810, S194 row 816, S195 row 814, Galway 2004, Hiary 2011, Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989. Cross-domain ingredient: Mellin-domain modulation of per-zero contributions (M_φ(ρ) = e^{ρ²h²/2} for log-Gaussian kernel) combined with GUE random-phase variance estimation — extends S195 GUE technique to a kernel-bandwidth axis, validated empirically across the full transition (h γ_K_max from 0 to 250). **Falsifier**: a non-Gaussian smoothing kernel for which K* drops to polylog; OR an empirical K*(h*, x, p) ≪ x at x ≥ 10⁹ for some h*. None observed in tested band. See `experiments/analytic/connes_amortisation/galway_smoothing.py`, `galway_smoothing_results.md`. | 196 |
    822:| S195 commit-thread step 3 (Connes amortisation, Galway frontier in distribution) — can the empirical decay of hit-rate at polylog K seen in S194 be matched by a closed-form heuristic prediction, and does that prediction extrapolate beyond the tested band? | THEORY+EXPERIMENT | E+I | Thread 2 commit step 3/5. **Slot 3 derives a GUE random-phase heuristic for σ²(K, x) := Var(π(x) − R_K(x))**: under the asymptotic equidistribution of {γ_j log x mod 2π} predicted by Montgomery (1973) pair-correlation and verified empirically by Odlyzko (1989), σ²(K, x) ≈ x · log²(K) / (2π² · K · log²(x)). Derivation: leading-order li-asymptotic gives 2 Re R(x^ρ) ≈ 2 x^{1/2} sin(γ log x)/(γ log x); under iid-phase model Var per pair = 2x/(γ_j² log² x); summing 1/γ_j² over j > K with γ_j ~ 2π j/log j yields log²(K)/(4π² K). **Empirical validation** against S194 fluctuation CSVs at x ~ 1e5, 1e6 plus a new S195 sweep at x ~ 1e7 (40 samples, K_max=5500): pooled across 600 (x, K, |err|) triples — Pearson r(σ_pred, |err|) = 0.6189; slope-through-origin 0.5901 vs half-Gaussian expected √(2/π) = 0.7979 (ratio 0.74). The 26% gap is the GUE-vs-Poisson variance reduction predicted by Dyson sine-kernel pair correlation. Per-policy median predictions match empirical within 5–55% across THREE decades of x: e.g. at K = log³(x), x = 1e7, predicted median |err| = 3.69 vs empirical 3.51 (within 5%); at K = log²(x), x = 1e6, predicted 5.21 vs empirical 4.56 (within 14%). **Asymptotic K* prediction**: closed-form binary search on σ ≤ threshold/(√2 · erfinv(p)) gives K*(x, p) ≈ 0.09·x for hit-rate p = 0.5 and K*(x, p) ≈ 1.35·x for p = 0.99, with K*/x → constant from above as x → ∞. **For ANY fixed positive hit-rate target, K*(x, p) = Θ(x), NOT polylog** — extends S194's tested-band negative-shape to all x via theoretically-grounded extrapolation. The heuristic does NOT contradict Galway 2004's smoothed-sum K = O(x^{1/2 + ε}) bound because Galway weights only nearby zeros heavily; for the unsmoothed Riemann series, the variance is dominated by the tail. Cites E3.1, E1.5, S193 row 810, S194 row 814, Galway 2004, Hiary 2011, Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989, Dyson sine-kernel. Cross-domain ingredient: GUE statistics of zero spacings + asymptotic li-expansion + Gaussian CLT applied to the truncation tail — first time the project has used random-matrix-theoretic equidistribution to PREDICT a quantitative empirical curve (not just bound it). **Falsifier**: a K-policy whose empirical median |err| stays bounded as x → ∞, OR a smoothing that drops the unsmoothed-K* below polylog, OR a rigorous calculation showing GUE correlation reduces K* below x · log^{-c}(x) for any c > 0. None known. See `experiments/analytic/connes_amortisation/gue_heuristic.py`, `gue_heuristic_results.md`. | 195 |
    871:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 5 (FINAL WRAP) — cross-conductor (Q-batched) amortisation: given Q = {q_1, ..., q_M} of distinct prime conductors, can the slot-3/4 pipeline share zero-finding work across distinct conductors to give per-(q, a) amortised polylog cost? | THEORY+EXPERIMENT (negative-shape closure of the fourth and final amortisation axis of P1) | E+I | Thread 6 commit step 5/5 FINAL (S231). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot5_cross_conductor_amortisation.py` (~400 lines): seven-stage decomposition of slot-3/4 pipeline (Stage A: cp_all = 1/n^{1/2+it} CONDUCTOR-INDEPENDENT; Stage B: aggregate cp_all by residue mod q PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage C: length-φ(q) FFT PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage D: reflected term + Gauss sums + W_χ PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage E: loggamma at t-grid CONDUCTOR-INDEPENDENT; Stage F: Hardy θ + Z PER-CONDUCTOR; Stage G: sign-change bracket PER-CONDUCTOR). Two pipelines: INDEPENDENT (rebuild Stage A+E inside q-loop) vs Q-BATCHED-SHARED (build Stage A sized to N_max + Stage E once before q-loop). **Numerical equivalence verified**: max abs diff between zero databases = 0.000e+00 at Q={101, 251, 503, 1009} K=5; Stage-A sharing introduces NO accuracy loss. **M-sweep K=200 n_t=823 t_max=200 family Q⊆{1009, 2003, 5003, 10007}**: M=1 (1009): T_indep 264.3ms vs T_batch 134.5ms, speedup 1.97× [ARTEFACT — pipelines functionally identical at M=1, ratio is cold-start FFT-planner / numpy allocation effect with indep called first per run-trio]; M=2: 485.5/389.7ms = 1.25×; M=3: 1267.4/1133.3ms = 1.12×; M=4: 3493.2/3341.4ms = 1.05×. **Speedup decreases monotonically toward 1× as M grows** — OPPOSITE shape from S224 Correlation Dichotomy (33× at M=64, INCREASING in M). Stage A breakdown M=4: indep_stageA_total=572ms, batch_stageA_once=343ms, saving 229ms (4.4% of total); Stage E saving 1ms (sub-dominant by 150×). **Theoretical Lemma (cross-conductor amortisation bounded constant)**: for Q = {q_1, ..., q_M} of distinct prime conductors, the slot-3-style pipeline admits T_batch/T_indep ≥ 1 - c·(T_A(N_max) + T_E)/T_indep(Q). Stages B, C, D, F, G all per-conductor by structural argument: (i) Stage B residue map n → n mod q is q-dependent; no primitive aggregates 1/n^{1/2+it} into ALL φ(q_i) characters of all q_i in Q simultaneously (distinct primes have COPRIME multiplicative groups (Z/qZ)* of order q-1 with no nontrivial common refinement; CRT (Z/q_1q_2)* ≅ (Z/q_1)* × (Z/q_2)* requires SINGLE composite modulus, not a *family*); (ii) Stages C, F, G have FFT length φ(q_i), q-dependent; (iii) Stage D has Gauss sums and root numbers depending on q. Saving is bounded by T_A(N_max) + T_E = O(N_max·n_t + n_t), one constant. Total per-conductor work grows as M·√q_avg·log q_avg·n_t. saving/total → 0 as M·√q_avg grows. ∎. **Corollary**: Q-axis admits NO Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped partial-positive on slot-3/4 architecture. **Thread 6 final verdict — P1 closes negatively across all four amortisation axes**: AXIS 1 (a-direction, slot 1, S227): trivial 8× at M=256 (zeros independent of a, exactly per E1.5); AXIS 2 (χ-direction fixed q, slots 2-3, S228+S229): bounded constant 1.79-2.04× via cyclic DFT primitive (BLAS-vs-Bluestein FFT FLOP rate); AXIS 3 (composite q, slot 4, S230): bounded constant 1.75× via multi-axis FFT (same crossover); AXIS 4 (Q-batched cross-conductor, slot 5, S231): bounded constant decreasing toward 1× as M grows. **None structural in algorithmic-complexity sense; all hardware-level FFT/BLAS arithmetic.** Thread 6 produces no batch-polylog primes-in-AP algorithm via explicit-formula route. **Thread-5-vs-Thread-6 distinction (NEW project artefact)**: Thread 5 partial-positive (S224) operated on SHARED-L geometry — same ζ-zero database across queries. Thread 6 fails on DISTINCT-L geometry — distinct L(s, χ_q) per query, no shared zero database across distinct conductors. Future P_x targets should prioritise shared-L shapes over distinct-L shapes when seeking partial-positives. **Successor thread recommendations (slot-5 self-extension)**: Thread 7 = P3 (approximate π(x) ± ε in polylog with named ε; smallest cross-domain barrier, strongest a-priori chance of partial-positive based on R(x) error analysis with K=log²x zeros heuristically yielding ε ≈ √x · log log x / log⁴ x); Thread 8 = P2 (π_h(x) batched on h; shared-h-singular-series structure closer to Thread 5 shape); Thread 9 = P9 (quantum batched primes-in-AP; possibly different scaling). **Falsifiers F5.1-F5.5**: F5.1 pipeline equivalence checked (max abs diff = 0e0); F5.2 speedup decreases monotonically (1.25 → 1.12 → 1.05) NOT sub-linear in M; F5.3 M=1 row 1.97× is timing artefact (cold-start FFT-planner with indep first per run); F5.4 ALTERNATIVE attack via Selberg trace formula or Iwaniec-Kowalski large-sieve NOT investigated (different cross-domain imports could change the lemma); F5.5 quantum batched amortisation NOT investigated (potential Thread 9). **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE NEW. Slot 5 is rigorous-experimentation + algebraic-structure argument applied to existing primitives (slot-3/4 architecture). The slot-5 result is theoretical lemma + empirical confirmation, NOT new technique import — appropriate for wrap session. CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §10 cyclic/multi-axis DFT family entries are now complete with the slot-5 negative-shape: family does NOT extend to a cross-conductor primitive. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; each axis confirms E1.5), E2.1 (spectral/sieve; cyclic and multi-axis DFT primitives), E3.1 (CCM setup-vs-eval decoupling applied to (A,E) vs (B-G)), E6.6 (Aggarwal binary search analogue), E6.7 (Deléglise-Rivat orthogonal pillar), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template; slot-5 produces Q-axis analogue with OPPOSITE shape), S190 (Thread 1 closed), S202 (Thread 3 closed), S215 (Thread 4 closed) — Thread 6 joins as fifth structural closure, S227-S230 (slots 1-4). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot5_cross_conductor_amortisation.py, slot5_cross_conductor_amortisation_results.md, slot5_cross_conductor.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session231_commit_p1_cross_conductor_wrap.md`. | 231 |
    876:| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 1 — partial-sum evaluator pi_K(x) = R(x) - 2 sum_{j<=K} Re R(x^{rho_j}) + named-exponent corollary: under Montgomery random-phase, what is the smallest eps(x) achievable in polylog time, named to explicit log-power exponent? | REFINEMENT (polylog-time partial-positive shape, named-exponent formula corrected from P3 entry, empirical extension S195 -> 5 decades) | E (E1.5 + S195 variance formula) — partial-positive (heuristic) | Thread 7 commit step 1/5 (S240). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/polylog_approx_pi.py` (parameterised K-policy evaluator across {1, log x, log^2 x, log^3 x, log^4 x, log^6 x, x^{1/4}, x^{1/2}}; uses S195's R_at_rho machinery at mp.dps=25, M=8 Mobius truncation). **Named-exponent corollary (heuristic, under Montgomery random-phase):** sigma(x, K = log^alpha x) ~ alpha * sqrt(x) * log log x / (pi sqrt(2) * log^{1 + alpha/2} x). Inverting: for any beta > 1, K = log^{2(beta-1)} x zeros yield polylog-time eps(x) <= sqrt(x) * log log x / log^beta x. **Empirical confirmation across 5 decades** at x in {10^5, 10^6, 10^7, 10^8, 10^10} with single-anchor canonical pi(10^k) values; full table of 35 (x, K) data points; median ratio empirical/sigma_pred = 0.476, mean 0.554 (consistent with half-Gaussian sqrt(2/pi) = 0.798 modulated by GUE pair-correlation reduction ~0.74 measured at S195); single-sample spread 0.07-1.66 within Gaussian-quantile band; no row exceeds 2sigma. **Headline polylog-better-than-sqrt(x) row**: at x=10^10, K=8000 (~ log^3 x), |err|=48.126 vs sqrt(x)=100000, ratio eps/sqrt(x) = 4.81e-4 — predicted 9.82e-4, ratio 0.49. Two new decades (10^8, 10^10) verify sigma-prediction beyond S195's 10^5-10^7 range. **Corrects** OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md §P3 formula: P3 originally claimed K=log^2 x gives eps ~ sqrt(x) * log log x / log^4 x (used K instead of sqrt(K) in denominator). Correct formula: K=log^2 x gives eps ~ sqrt(x) * log log x / log^2 x; to attain eps ~ sqrt(x) / log^4 x need K = log^6 x zeros. **Distinction from S195/Thread 3**: S195 closed Thread 3 ("polylog K -> threshold hit-rate -> 0 -> exact pi(x) hopeless in polylog"); Thread 7 slot 1 RE-FRAMES the same variance formula to ask the complementary algorithmic question ("polylog K -> what is the smallest typical eps?") and gives a positive named-exponent answer. Two complementary results: threshold negative + quantitative positive. **Open vs A-grade gap**: result is heuristic (random-phase model unproven); single-sample-per-decade rather than 20+ samples; "polylog improvement" is log-factor not x-factor. Slot 2 should run multi-sample averaging at x in {10^9..10^12} to tighten empirical confirmation; theoretical extrapolation to x = 10^15, 10^20 via S195's predictor; verify named-exponent corollary at extreme x. **Cross-domain ingredient**: re-uses S195's GUE / random-phase heuristic (Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md updated to mark this as a complementary algorithmic re-framing of the variance formula. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier), E2.1 (spectral / sieve), E3.1 (CCM spectral triple), S195 (variance formula), S196 (Galway frontier closure conditional on random-phase), S202 (unified CCM closure), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy partial-positive template Thread 7 follows). See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{polylog_approx_pi.py, polylog_approx_pi_results.md, polylog_approx_pi_main.csv, main_run.log}`, `archive/sessions/session240_commit_p3_partial_sum_evaluator.md`. | 240 |
    877:| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 2 — multi-sample empirical validation of the S195 sigma-formula at three anchors x in {10^7, 10^8, 10^9}, with proper KS-statistic distribution test and theoretical extrapolation table to x = 10^24 | REFINEMENT (extends S195 multi-sample data from 10^7 to 10^9; confirms half-Gaussian shape under sigma_eff scaling) | E (E1.5 + S195 + S240) — partial-positive (heuristic; refines slot-1 confidence) | Thread 7 commit step 2/5 (S241). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/multi_sample.py` (~360 lines): N=30 geometric samples per anchor in [x_0, x_0*sqrt(10)] (half-decade window, log-step ~0.040 vs decoherence length 2pi/gamma_K ~ 7.7e-4 at K_max=8000 -> ~52x decoherent); chunked segmented Eratosthenes sieve `pi_array_for_samples(x_anchor, pi_anchor, sample_xs, chunk_size=10^8)` using bytearray + numpy.cumsum (caps memory at ~100MB; x_anchor=10^9 sieves half-decade in 41s); per-sample R_at_rho cumulative loop K=1..K_max with policy reads at K in {1, log x, log^2 x, log^3 x, K_max}; aggregate stats with TWO KS tests — KS_pred (|err|/sigma_pred vs half-normal CDF, tests S195's full prediction) and KS_eff (|err|/sigma_eff vs half-normal CDF, tests SHAPE only after rescaling by empirical rms; absorbs S195's GUE 0.74 factor). **Headline result (12 (anchor, policy) cells, 30 samples each, 450 (x, policy) data points total)**: half-Gaussian SHAPE confirmed under sigma_eff rescaling (median KS_p_eff = 0.69 across 9 cells with K >= log^2 x; cells with K_p_pred < 0.05 e.g. 10^9 K=8000 KS_p_pred=0.009 BUT KS_p_eff=0.695, the rejection is purely scale not shape). **GUE 0.74 factor stable**: sigma_eff/sigma_pred = {10^7: 0.756, 10^8: 0.644, 10^9: 0.620} at log^2 x; {10^7: 0.746, 10^8: 0.796, 10^9: 0.754} at log^3 x; {10^7: 0.904, 10^8: 0.781, 10^9: 0.754} at K_max=8000. Median across 9 (anchor, policy >= log^2 x) cells: 0.755 (S195 reported pooled 0.74 at x in {10^5..10^7}; extension to 10^9 confirms within +/-5%). **eps/sqrt(x) at K=8000 fixed, 1/log x decay**: 9.74e-4 (10^7) -> 8.84e-4 (10^8) -> 7.81e-4 (10^9), cross-decade ratio 1.25 vs predicted log(10^9)/log(10^7) = 1.286 — agreement within 3%. **Empirical anomaly at small K**: logx policy (K = 17, 19, 22) gives sigma_eff/sigma_pred = {0.734, 0.599, 0.816} much noisier than larger-K policies — expected because random-phase asymptotic regime breaks at K below ~50 (sigma_pred drops the K small-zero terms wholesale). **Theoretical extrapolation table** (sigma_pred for K = log^alpha x at x in {10^6, 10^8, 10^10, 10^12, 10^15, 10^18, 10^21, 10^24}, alpha in {2, 3, 4, 6, 8}): at x = 10^15 with K = log^4(10^15) ~ 9.3e5 zeros, sigma/sqrt(x) ~ 7.7e-5 -> polylog-time algorithm with eps ~ 2400 vs R(x) baseline O(sqrt(x)) ~ 3.2e7 (13000x improvement, heuristic). **Distinction from slot 1**: slot 1 was 5 single-anchor data points; slot 2 is 90 multi-sample points = 360 (x, policy) data triples. Slot 1 measured median ratio 0.476; slot 2 confirms half-Gaussian shape (KS_p_eff median 0.69) AND extends to one new decade (10^9, no prior data; S195 stopped at 10^7). **Falsifiability**: (1) multi-sample run at x >= 10^12 with KS_p_eff < 0.01 across multiple policies; (2) rigorous proof of GUE pair-correlation factor < 0.74^2 = 0.55 (would imply sigma_eff smaller than measured); (3) K-policy below logx where asymptotic formula breaks measurably (already documented at K = 17-22). **No A-grade**: still validates S195's heuristic; doesn't produce a new theorem or beat an algorithm; the partial-positive shape is unchanged from slot 1, just with more confidence and one new decade. **Cross-domain ingredient**: GUE pair-correlation distribution (Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989) extended from USED-E (S195) to USED-E + USED-I (slot 2 inverts to give algorithmic ratio bound). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md unchanged but slot 2 anchor row strengthens the empirical evidence. **Cites** E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier; sigma_eff bounds typical error log-factor below sqrt(x)), E2.1, E3.1 (Thread 3 closure transitivity now backed by 360-point data extending to 10^9), S195 (variance formula re-validated at 10^9), S196 (Galway frontier closure conditional on same heuristic), S202 (unified CCM closure), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template Thread 7 follows), S240 (slot 1 named-exponent corollary). **Slot 3 next-action**: push empirical anchor to x = 10^12 via memory-efficient sieve (bit-packed segmented sieve, or primality-test-only approach in narrow sample windows where pi(anchor) is precomputable); OR pivot to slot 4 (smoothed kernel selection: Gaussian / raised-cosine kernels — do they reduce eps beyond unsmoothed sum?). See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{multi_sample.py, multi_sample_results.md, multi_sample_data.csv, multi_sample_summary.csv, multi_sample_main.log}`, `archive/sessions/session241_commit_p3_multisample.md`. | 241 |
