    877:| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 3 — do compactly-supported smoothing kernels (Hann, Hamming, Triangle, Riesz, Riesz4, Tukey-25, Tukey-50, Cosine) beat hard cutoff for sigma_eff at fixed K_compute? Resolves the S202 wrap §"Non-Gaussian smoothing kernels" legitimate-falsifier listing | EXPERIMENT (negative-shape closure of the kernel-axis falsifier of slot-1 named-exponent corollary; empirical L2-optimality of hard cutoff for the partial-sum approximation) | E (E1.5 + S195 + S196 + S202 + S240 + S241) — confirms hard cutoff is L2-optimal, no compactly-supported kernel reduces sigma_eff | Thread 7 commit step 3/5 (S242). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/smoothed_kernels.py` (~340 lines): per-sample cumulative loop computing c_k = 2 Re R(x^{rho_k}) for k=1..K_max=4000 (one R_at_rho call per (sample, k)), then numpy-dot post-processing for each (kernel, K_compute) combination — 9 kernels, 4 K-targets, 20 paired samples, 3 anchors x in {10^7, 10^8, 10^9}. Kernels: hard (w_k=1), triangle (1-u), hann ((1+cos pi u)/2), hamming (0.54+0.46 cos pi u), riesz (1-u^2), riesz4 ((1-u^2)^2), tukey25 (hard for u<=0.75 then cosine taper), tukey50 (hard for u<=0.5 then cosine taper), cosine (cos(pi u/2)) where u = (k-1)/(K-1) in [0,1]. **Headline grid (108 cells, 2160 (anchor, x, K, kernel) data triples)**: zero (anchor, K, kernel) cells where sigma_eff(kernel) < sigma_eff(hard) at paired sign-test p < 0.05 (one-sided). Mean sigma_eff/sigma_eff(hard) across 12 (anchor, K) cells, by kernel: tukey25=1.04, tukey50=1.11, cosine=1.14, riesz=1.12, triangle=1.23, riesz4=1.21, hamming=1.20, hann=1.23 (all > 1). **Decisive hard wins** at 10^8 K=2000+4000 and 10^9 K=2000+4000: ratios 1.06 to 1.62, paired sign test 12-17/20 hard wins, p < 0.01 in 4 cells. **Sub-1 ratios** at K=500 and one tukey25 cell at K=1000 (10^7): all within 1-sigma noise (CV(sigma_eff) at N=20 is ~16%); none significant (smallest p_kernel_wins = 0.13 at 10^7/K=1000/tukey25). Asymmetry: 16 of 24 ratios >= 1.05 are 1-sigma above 1.0; 0 of 9 sub-1 ratios are 1-sigma below 1.0. **Closes S202 wrap §"Non-Gaussian smoothing kernels" legitimate-falsifier**: S196 closed log-Gaussian (multiplicative damping w_j=exp(-h^2 gamma_j^2/2), all j), S202 wrap explicitly listed compactly-supported tapers as "should generalise but not formally proven". Slot 3 directly tests and confirms — 0 of 96 cells show kernel-beats-hard at p<0.05. **Structural reason**: under random-phase Var(error) = sum_j (1-w_j)^2 Var_j is minimised at w_j=1 (j<=K), 0 (j>K), the hard cutoff. The 0.74 GUE factor scales sigma_eff uniformly across kernels (verified empirically: 5 of 5 anchor/K cells with K >= log^2(x) and hard show sigma_eff/sigma_pred in [0.62, 0.85]); GUE corrections do NOT discriminate between kernels at the second-moment level. **Implication for slot-1 named-exponent corollary**: the bound sigma ~ sqrt(x) log K / (pi sqrt(2K) log x) is kernel-optimal in this family; to beat it requires either (a) non-symmetric / position-correlated kernels (paired weights w_{2j-1}=w_{2j} exploiting Wigner repulsion; slot 4 candidate); (b) non-linear post-processing of {c_k} (empirical Bayes shrinkage; outside slot-3 family); (c) move beyond explicit-formula partial-sum framework entirely (slot-5 territory with rigorous variance proof). Slot 3 closes the kernel-optimisation pathway as a route to A-grade. **Cross-domain ingredient**: window-function theory (Bartlett, Hann, Hamming, Tukey, Riesz from signal processing); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md should reflect "compactly-supported window functions for explicit-formula partial-sum smoothing — USED-E (S242) — confirmed no improvement over hard cutoff for the partial-sum approximation pi_K(x)". **Falsifier**: (1) a compactly-supported kernel yielding sigma_eff(kernel)/sigma_eff(hard) < 0.95 with paired sign-test p<0.01 at any (anchor, K) cell — directly tested, ruled out; (2) non-symmetric / position-correlated kernel structure — slot-4 candidate; (3) second-moment GUE correction calculation showing kernel-dependent cross-zero covariance — theoretical, outside current machinery. **Cites** E1.5, E3.1, S195, S196, S202, S240, S241. See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{smoothed_kernels.py, smoothed_kernels_results.md, smoothed_kernels_data.csv (2160 rows), smoothed_kernels_summary.csv (108 rows), smoothed_kernels_main.log, smoothed_kernels_paired_analysis.py, smoothed_kernels_paired.log}`, `archive/sessions/session242_commit_p3_smoothed_kernels.md`. | 242 |
    878:| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 2 — multi-sample empirical validation of the S195 sigma-formula at three anchors x in {10^7, 10^8, 10^9}, with proper KS-statistic distribution test and theoretical extrapolation table to x = 10^24 | REFINEMENT (extends S195 multi-sample data from 10^7 to 10^9; confirms half-Gaussian shape under sigma_eff scaling) | E (E1.5 + S195 + S240) — partial-positive (heuristic; refines slot-1 confidence) | Thread 7 commit step 2/5 (S241). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/multi_sample.py` (~360 lines): N=30 geometric samples per anchor in [x_0, x_0*sqrt(10)] (half-decade window, log-step ~0.040 vs decoherence length 2pi/gamma_K ~ 7.7e-4 at K_max=8000 -> ~52x decoherent); chunked segmented Eratosthenes sieve `pi_array_for_samples(x_anchor, pi_anchor, sample_xs, chunk_size=10^8)` using bytearray + numpy.cumsum (caps memory at ~100MB; x_anchor=10^9 sieves half-decade in 41s); per-sample R_at_rho cumulative loop K=1..K_max with policy reads at K in {1, log x, log^2 x, log^3 x, K_max}; aggregate stats with TWO KS tests — KS_pred (|err|/sigma_pred vs half-normal CDF, tests S195's full prediction) and KS_eff (|err|/sigma_eff vs half-normal CDF, tests SHAPE only after rescaling by empirical rms; absorbs S195's GUE 0.74 factor). **Headline result (12 (anchor, policy) cells, 30 samples each, 450 (x, policy) data points total)**: half-Gaussian SHAPE confirmed under sigma_eff rescaling (median KS_p_eff = 0.69 across 9 cells with K >= log^2 x; cells with K_p_pred < 0.05 e.g. 10^9 K=8000 KS_p_pred=0.009 BUT KS_p_eff=0.695, the rejection is purely scale not shape). **GUE 0.74 factor stable**: sigma_eff/sigma_pred = {10^7: 0.756, 10^8: 0.644, 10^9: 0.620} at log^2 x; {10^7: 0.746, 10^8: 0.796, 10^9: 0.754} at log^3 x; {10^7: 0.904, 10^8: 0.781, 10^9: 0.754} at K_max=8000. Median across 9 (anchor, policy >= log^2 x) cells: 0.755 (S195 reported pooled 0.74 at x in {10^5..10^7}; extension to 10^9 confirms within +/-5%). **eps/sqrt(x) at K=8000 fixed, 1/log x decay**: 9.74e-4 (10^7) -> 8.84e-4 (10^8) -> 7.81e-4 (10^9), cross-decade ratio 1.25 vs predicted log(10^9)/log(10^7) = 1.286 — agreement within 3%. **Empirical anomaly at small K**: logx policy (K = 17, 19, 22) gives sigma_eff/sigma_pred = {0.734, 0.599, 0.816} much noisier than larger-K policies — expected because random-phase asymptotic regime breaks at K below ~50 (sigma_pred drops the K small-zero terms wholesale). **Theoretical extrapolation table** (sigma_pred for K = log^alpha x at x in {10^6, 10^8, 10^10, 10^12, 10^15, 10^18, 10^21, 10^24}, alpha in {2, 3, 4, 6, 8}): at x = 10^15 with K = log^4(10^15) ~ 9.3e5 zeros, sigma/sqrt(x) ~ 7.7e-5 -> polylog-time algorithm with eps ~ 2400 vs R(x) baseline O(sqrt(x)) ~ 3.2e7 (13000x improvement, heuristic). **Distinction from slot 1**: slot 1 was 5 single-anchor data points; slot 2 is 90 multi-sample points = 360 (x, policy) data triples. Slot 1 measured median ratio 0.476; slot 2 confirms half-Gaussian shape (KS_p_eff median 0.69) AND extends to one new decade (10^9, no prior data; S195 stopped at 10^7). **Falsifiability**: (1) multi-sample run at x >= 10^12 with KS_p_eff < 0.01 across multiple policies; (2) rigorous proof of GUE pair-correlation factor < 0.74^2 = 0.55 (would imply sigma_eff smaller than measured); (3) K-policy below logx where asymptotic formula breaks measurably (already documented at K = 17-22). **No A-grade**: still validates S195's heuristic; doesn't produce a new theorem or beat an algorithm; the partial-positive shape is unchanged from slot 1, just with more confidence and one new decade. **Cross-domain ingredient**: GUE pair-correlation distribution (Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989) extended from USED-E (S195) to USED-E + USED-I (slot 2 inverts to give algorithmic ratio bound). CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md unchanged but slot 2 anchor row strengthens the empirical evidence. **Cites** E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier; sigma_eff bounds typical error log-factor below sqrt(x)), E2.1, E3.1 (Thread 3 closure transitivity now backed by 360-point data extending to 10^9), S195 (variance formula re-validated at 10^9), S196 (Galway frontier closure conditional on same heuristic), S202 (unified CCM closure), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template Thread 7 follows), S240 (slot 1 named-exponent corollary). **Slot 3 next-action**: push empirical anchor to x = 10^12 via memory-efficient sieve (bit-packed segmented sieve, or primality-test-only approach in narrow sample windows where pi(anchor) is precomputable); OR pivot to slot 4 (smoothed kernel selection: Gaussian / raised-cosine kernels — do they reduce eps beyond unsmoothed sum?). See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{multi_sample.py, multi_sample_results.md, multi_sample_data.csv, multi_sample_summary.csv, multi_sample_main.log}`, `archive/sessions/session241_commit_p3_multisample.md`. | 241 |
