    876	| §P.P3 polylog approximation of pi(x) with named-exponent error, slot 1 — partial-sum evaluator pi_K(x) = R(x) - 2 sum_{j<=K} Re R(x^{rho_j}) + named-exponent corollary: under Montgomery random-phase, what is the smallest eps(x) achievable in polylog time, named to explicit log-power exponent? | REFINEMENT (polylog-time partial-positive shape, named-exponent formula corrected from P3 entry, empirical extension S195 -> 5 decades) | E (E1.5 + S195 variance formula) — partial-positive (heuristic) | Thread 7 commit step 1/5 (S240). Built `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/polylog_approx_pi.py` (parameterised K-policy evaluator across {1, log x, log^2 x, log^3 x, log^4 x, log^6 x, x^{1/4}, x^{1/2}}; uses S195's R_at_rho machinery at mp.dps=25, M=8 Mobius truncation). **Named-exponent corollary (heuristic, under Montgomery random-phase):** sigma(x, K = log^alpha x) ~ alpha * sqrt(x) * log log x / (pi sqrt(2) * log^{1 + alpha/2} x). Inverting: for any beta > 1, K = log^{2(beta-1)} x zeros yield polylog-time eps(x) <= sqrt(x) * log log x / log^beta x. **Empirical confirmation across 5 decades** at x in {10^5, 10^6, 10^7, 10^8, 10^10} with single-anchor canonical pi(10^k) values; full table of 35 (x, K) data points; median ratio empirical/sigma_pred = 0.476, mean 0.554 (consistent with half-Gaussian sqrt(2/pi) = 0.798 modulated by GUE pair-correlation reduction ~0.74 measured at S195); single-sample spread 0.07-1.66 within Gaussian-quantile band; no row exceeds 2sigma. **Headline polylog-better-than-sqrt(x) row**: at x=10^10, K=8000 (~ log^3 x), |err|=48.126 vs sqrt(x)=100000, ratio eps/sqrt(x) = 4.81e-4 — predicted 9.82e-4, ratio 0.49. Two new decades (10^8, 10^10) verify sigma-prediction beyond S195's 10^5-10^7 range. **Corrects** OPEN_POSITIVE_TARGETS.md §P3 formula: P3 originally claimed K=log^2 x gives eps ~ sqrt(x) * log log x / log^4 x (used K instead of sqrt(K) in denominator). Correct formula: K=log^2 x gives eps ~ sqrt(x) * log log x / log^2 x; to attain eps ~ sqrt(x) / log^4 x need K = log^6 x zeros. **Distinction from S195/Thread 3**: S195 closed Thread 3 ("polylog K -> threshold hit-rate -> 0 -> exact pi(x) hopeless in polylog"); Thread 7 slot 1 RE-FRAMES the same variance formula to ask the complementary algorithmic question ("polylog K -> what is the smallest typical eps?") and gives a positive named-exponent answer. Two complementary results: threshold negative + quantitative positive. **Open vs A-grade gap**: result is heuristic (random-phase model unproven); single-sample-per-decade rather than 20+ samples; "polylog improvement" is log-factor not x-factor. Slot 2 should run multi-sample averaging at x in {10^9..10^12} to tighten empirical confirmation; theoretical extrapolation to x = 10^15, 10^20 via S195's predictor; verify named-exponent corollary at extreme x. **Cross-domain ingredient**: re-uses S195's GUE / random-phase heuristic (Montgomery 1973, Odlyzko 1989); CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md updated to mark this as a complementary algorithmic re-framing of the variance formula. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content barrier), E2.1 (spectral / sieve), E3.1 (CCM spectral triple), S195 (variance formula), S196 (Galway frontier closure conditional on random-phase), S202 (unified CCM closure), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy partial-positive template Thread 7 follows). See `experiments/analytic/polylog_approx_pi/{polylog_approx_pi.py, polylog_approx_pi_results.md, polylog_approx_pi_main.csv, main_run.log}`, `archive/sessions/session240_commit_p3_partial_sum_evaluator.md`. | 240 |
